// Benchmark rates of unemployment for the data.html website charts.
// The u*_PM column is generated by Research/SR and LR U star/replication-programs/
//   U-LR/export_website_benchmarks.py — do not hand-edit it. The other columns are
//   still updated by hand each quarter (NPNs Data Tools/_ops/DATA_UPDATE_RUNBOOK.md),
//   and are pinned against website_benchmarks_baseline.json so this script cannot
//   change them without being told to.
//
// US: [date, UNRATE, u*_SP preferred, CBO NROU, u*_PM], quarterly 1985:Q1-2026:Q1.
// CA: [date, unemployment rate, u*_SP preferred, U-LR model], quarterly 1990:Q1-2026:Q1.
//     U-SP begins 2000:Q2 (exchange-rate regressor binds); U-LR is the model estimate.
//     Canada has no U-PM column: the potential minimum is published for the US only.
//
// u*_PM is the potential minimum (u*_LR approach 3), k = 8 year horizon under the
// "hold" end-of-sample rule. This is the author's calculation, NOT a result in the
// source paper, which extrapolates instead and gives 2.94% at 2026:Q1 where this
// gives 3.58%. The recursion needs an eight-year look-ahead, so the last quarter it
// estimates is 2018:Q2; each of the 120 demographic cells then keeps its estimated floor
// from that quarter and the aggregate moves only with labor-force shares (a net
// +0.02pp to 2026:Q1, though the path dips to 3.46 in 2020:Q2 as composition swings).
// Source: output_uLR.xlsx, column uPMU_k8_hold.

